\ Spitfire: a Supermarine Spitfire Mk I over Scotland in 1940, in
\ wireframe. Every armed mission but the flying lesson may be flown in a
\ Hawker Hurricane Mk I instead, chosen on its briefing.
\
\   ./telic examples/spitfire.telic
\
\ The title lists the missions: the Ju 88 raid on the Forth of 16 October
\ 1939, from the runway at RAF Turnhouse, west of Edinburgh; the raid on the
\ Home Fleet in Scapa Flow of 16 March 1940, He 111s from the east, flown in
\ a Hurricane from Wick on patrol over the Pentland Firth; Narvik on 28
\ May 1940, the day the Allies retook the town, flown only in 46 Squadron's
\ Hurricane from Bardufoss against Kampfgruppe 100's He 111s coming for the
\ cruisers and destroyers in the Ofotfjord; HMS Glorious on 7 June 1940,
\ a Hurricane of 46 Squadron, with no hook and a sandbag in its tail,
\ landing on her flight deck as she steams into the wind north of Andenes,
\ braking to rest on the moving deck; the dogfight
\ off the Farne Islands of 15 August 1940, begun among the Bf 110s escorting
\ KG 26's He 111s to Tyneside, which break toward a Spitfire behind them and
\ turn their nose guns onto one ahead, with five more of 72 Squadron's
\ Spitfires fighting beside the player's; the night raids
\ on Clydebank of 13 March 1941 and on Greenock of 6 May 1941, from a patrol
\ over the Clyde; the photographic reconnaissance of 5 March 1942, the
\ unarmed PR IV from RAF Wick 580 miles to Trondheim to photograph the
\ Tirpitz in Fættenfjord, its film developed and reviewed after landing;
\ a flying lesson from Turnhouse, an instructor in the back seat talking
\ the pupil step by step through the take-off, the propeller's pitch, the
\ climb, trim, turns, the stall, flaps and the landing; air firing over the
\ Forth, live rounds at a drogue towed by a Lysander, the instructor taking
\ the pupil through ranging, astern and quarter attacks and a turning
\ target and assessing every burst; free flight from
\ Turnhouse; and landing practice on the
\ approach to Abbotsinch, in clear air and in fog. Each mission has its
\ weather: the visibility, and cumulus at a share of the sky and a base
\ height, which hide what lies behind them and blind the pilot inside them.
\ The map runs
\ from the Clyde to the Firth of Forth, south-east to the Farne Islands
\ and Tyneside, north up the Great Glen to
\ Inverness, up the east coast by Montrose, Aberdeen and Peterhead, on over
\ Wick to Orkney and Shetland, placed by the azimuthal equidistant projection
\ from 56°N 3.6°W, true in distance and bearing from there, and, across 580 miles of the North Sea placed at
\ their true distance and bearing, round the Trondheimsfjord: Trondheim
\ with Nidaros Cathedral, Munkholmen and its harbour, the U-boat bunker Dora I
\ half built, the Luftwaffe's airfield at Værnes, and the Tirpitz with two
\ destroyers behind their nets in Fættenfjord, over the surveyed hills round
\ the fjord. In Scotland: Edinburgh's castle on its rock, St Giles', the Hub, the Tron,
\ the Scott Monument, the Balmoral Hotel, Waverley and the North Bridge,
\ Calton Hill's monuments, St Mary's Cathedral and Fettes College; the Forth
\ Bridge; Glasgow's University on Gilmorehill, Kelvingrove, Trinity
\ College's towers, the Cathedral and the Necropolis, the City Chambers, the
\ Tolbooth Steeple, the Finnieston and Titan cranes and the shipyards;
\ Stirling, Loch
\ Lomond and Loch Ness, the Highlands, the Pentlands, Ochils and Campsies on
\ the surveyed ground, and the
\ airfields at Turnhouse, Drem, Abbotsinch, Dalcross, Wick, Castletown,
\ Dyce and Montrose. In the north-east: Aberdeen's granite streets, Marischal
\ College's Mitchell Tower, the Town House, the Kirk of St Nicholas, King's
\ College Chapel and St Machar's Cathedral; Peterhead's prison; Montrose's
\ steeple; the lighthouses at Girdle Ness, Buchan Ness and Scurdie Ness.
\ In Orkney: Kirkwall's streets, St Magnus Cathedral and the palaces beside
\ it, the Home Fleet at anchor in Scapa Flow, the oil tanks at Lyness, the
\ Old Man of Hoy, the Ring of Brodgar, the Kitchener Memorial, and the
\ airfields at Hatston and Skeabrae. In Shetland: Lerwick's streets, Fort
\ Charlotte, the Town Hall, Scalloway Castle, the lighthouses on Sumburgh
\ Head and Muckle Flugga, and RAF Sumburgh. Round Narvik, the
\ Ofotfjord, Rombaksfjord and Herjangsfjord among their mountains, the
\ town's streets, quays and ore railway, the Allied ships off it, the
\ wrecks of the destroyers Georg Thiele and Hermann Künne on the shore, and
\ Bardufoss airfield 77 m up in the valley to the north. Each mission has a
\ wind, which carries the aircraft over the ground, and gusts, stronger near
\ the ground in a strong wind, under the cumulus and inside it.
\
\ Keys: the arrows are the stick (down pulls back, lifting the nose; left and
\ right roll), Z and X the rudder, M the magnetos on and off, K held the
\ starter (on the runway the engine starts stopped), O held the
\ slow-running cut-out that stops it, Q and A the throttle, [ and ] the
\ elevator trim nose-down and nose-up, W and S the propeller finer and
\ coarser, E the boost cut-out, C the cameras on the reconnaissance
\ sortie, T time four and sixteen times as fast, V the film's review after
\ landing, F the flaps, G the
\ undercarriage, B the brakes, space the guns, J bails out (the hood back,
\ then over the side, then the ripcord), N held the chart on the pilot's
\ knee across the whole screen (the mission's map without the marks that
\ move, in the detail of a printed one: the land tinted by height, rivers,
\ railways, towns, airfields, summits and ten-mile squares; the aircraft
\ flying on unseen), comma and period the rudder trim
\ left and right, in the Hurricane R the fuel cock, 1 to 4 look ahead, left, right
\ and behind, H the help (the keys, the cockpit, flying, fighting and finding
\ the way, left and right turning its pages, the flight paused), P pauses,
\ escape returns to the
\ missions. A gamepad: the left stick is the stick, the shoulders the rudder,
\ the right stick's up and down or the d-pad's the throttle, the right
\ stick's left and right the trim, the stick clicks the propeller (right
\ finer, left coarser), the left trigger the boost cut-out, A or the right
\ trigger the guns, X the flaps, Y the undercarriage, B the brakes, the d-pad
\ left and right turns the view, start pauses and back returns to the
\ missions; on the ground with the engine stopped A switches the magnetos
\ on and, held, turns the starter, and at rest with it running the left
\ trigger held stops it. On the title, up and down or the d-pad choose a mission, return,
\ start or A opens its briefing (a map of the area, the particulars and the
\ orders), where return, start or A flies it; H or Y shows the keys; escape
\ quits. The two campaigns at the head of the list, the Defence of Scotland
\ and Norway, fly their missions in order; after each a debrief says what
\ happened, and a mission not flown, the pilot lost or the task undone, is
\ flown again. Their progress is kept in ~/.telic-spitfire-campaigns.
\
\ The flight model, its figures and their sources are in
\ examples/spitfire/flight.telic; the ground's heights and Trondheim's
\ harbour in norway-terrain.telic, generated by tools/gen-norway-terrain.py
\ from the AWS Terrain Tiles (Norway terrain data © Kartverket) and from
\ OpenStreetMap; the land round Narvik and Bardufoss, its heights, coast,
\ landmarks, streets, quays and railways, in narvik.telic, generated by
\ tools/gen-narvik.py from the same tiles, OpenStreetMap and Wikidata;
\ Scotland's heights in scotland-terrain.telic, generated by
\ tools/gen-scotland-terrain.py from the same tiles (produced using
\ Copernicus data and information funded by the European Union - EU-DEM
\ layers; SRTM data courtesy of the U.S. Geological Survey; © Environment
\ Agency copyright and/or database right 2015);
\ the map in geography.telic, generated by
\ tools/gen-spitfire-map.py from Natural Earth and Wikidata, and Glasgow's,
\ Edinburgh's and Inverness's streets, squares, stations, railways and some
\ buildings' footprints from OpenStreetMap
\ (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL), and every Scottish town's other
\ streets in town-streets.telic, generated beside it; the scenery in
\ world.telic, cities.telic and shapes.telic; the view in render.telic; the clouds in
\ clouds.telic; the cameras, the film's review and the flak in
\ reconnaissance.telic; the lesson's steps in lesson.telic; the air firing in
\ air-firing.telic; the briefings in briefing.telic; bailing out in
\ parachute.telic; the panel's
\ stroke lettering in lettering.telic; the
\ instruments in
\ cockpit.telic; the aircraft's models, the Spitfire's and the Hurricane's
\ among them, in aircraft.telic; the Spitfire's, the PR IV's and the
\ Hurricane's figures for the flight model in aircraft-types.telic; the
\ sound in sound.telic, and with it the music under the title and the
\ briefings: Paul Whiteman and his Ambassador Orchestra's "Anytime, Anyday,
\ Anywhere" (Victor 18694, 1920, the Library of Congress's National
\ Jukebox transfer), in examples/assets/anytime-anyday-anywhere.mp3,
\ decoded through ffmpeg; the game in game.telic.

"spitfire/geography.telic" load
"spitfire/town-streets.telic" load
"spitfire/norway-terrain.telic" load
"spitfire/narvik.telic" load
